---

`oriel.tts` reads text aloud offline with Kokoro-82M, on every platform:
a catalog of models and voices with verified downloads, Markdown read as
prose, the language guessed from the text, and streaming playback that
starts after the first sentence. It is the counterpart of
[`oriel.chat` and `oriel.dictation`]($link.page('docs/modules')). The building blocks,
`oriel.kokoro` (synthesis) and `oriel.audio_play` (playback), stay public.

# Quick start

Enable Kokoro in your app's `build.zig`, alongside llama or whisper (they
supply the shared GGML source tree):

```zig
const dep = b.dependency("oriel", .{
    .target = target,
    .optimize = optimize,
    .kokoro = true,
    .whisper = true, // or .llama = true
});
```

Then, in `main`:

```zig
oriel.tts.init(init.io, init.gpa, models_dir);   // once, at startup (e.g. store.dataDir + "tts")
try oriel.tts.download("kokoro-82m-q8_0");        // from a worker; once per device
try oriel.tts.download("af_heart");               // a voice (0.5 MB)
const r = try oriel.tts.speak(gpa, "# Hello\nRead **this** aloud.", .{}); // from a worker
// r.first_audio_ms, r.synth_ms / 1000 / r.audio_s (the real-time factor), r.gap_ms...
oriel.tts.stop();                                 // Stop, from any thread
```

Expose it to the page with commands of your own, and forward the module's
events through your app's `Events` struct:

```zig
pub const Events = struct {
    @"tts:download": @FieldType(oriel.tts.Events, "tts:download"),
    @"tts:state": @FieldType(oriel.tts.Events, "tts:state"),
};

pub const Commands = struct {
    pub const async_commands = .{ "speak", "tts_download", "tts_warm_up" };
    pub fn speak(gpa: std.mem.Allocator, args: struct { text: []const u8, options: oriel.tts.Options = .{} }) !oriel.tts.Result {
        return oriel.tts.speak(gpa, args.text, args.options);
    }
    pub fn tts_download(_: std.mem.Allocator, args: struct { id: []const u8 }) !void {
        try oriel.tts.download(args.id);
    }
    pub fn tts_warm_up(_: std.mem.Allocator, args: oriel.tts.Options) !void {
        try oriel.tts.warmUp(args);
    }
    // Not async: it must run while `speak` is busy on a worker.
    pub fn tts_stop(_: std.mem.Allocator) void {
        oriel.tts.stop();
    }
};
```

`examples/showcase`'s Speak tab and its "Read aloud" buttons are built this
way; see [its README](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel/blob/main/examples/showcase/README.md#speak-text-to-speech).

# Build options

| Option | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `.kokoro = true` / `-Dkokoro` | Kokoro (kokoro.cpp), espeak-ng phonemization, Highway SIMD and miniaudio; `oriel.tts`, `oriel.kokoro`, `oriel.audio_play`. Needs `.llama` or `.whisper`. Its dependencies are fetched lazily. |
| `.tts_languages = "en,es,..."` | The espeak-ng dictionaries compiled into the bundled phoneme data (default `en,es,fr,pt,it,ja,cmn,hi`; see Bundled phoneme data below). |
| `-Dggml_cuda` | Kokoro on NVIDIA GPUs (Linux; with llama/whisper's `libggml-cuda.so`). |
| `-Dggml_vulkan` | Kokoro on Vulkan GPUs (with `libggml-vulkan.so`; on Windows and Android, a backend registered only when a Vulkan loader exists). |
| `-Dggml_metal` | Kokoro on Metal (on by default for macOS and iOS devices, off for the simulator). |

The GPU flags are the same ones that put llama and whisper on the GPU
([GPU backends]($link.page('docs/modules'))). Kokoro's
calls into the CUDA and Vulkan backends go through ggml's backend registry,
so an app without a GPU, or without the backend library, stays on the CPU.

From the Oriel checkout, the TTS-enabled regression suite runs with
`zig build test -Dkokoro -Dwhisper -Daudio_capture=false`
(see Validation below).

# Models, voices and downloads

| Catalog | |
| --- | --- |
| Models (`oriel.tts.models`) | `kokoro-82m-q8_0` (135 MB, the default) and `kokoro-82m-f16` (156 MB) |
| Voices (`oriel.tts.voices`, 0.5 MB each) | English (US): `af_heart`, `af_bella`, `af_nicole`, `am_michael`, `am_fenrir`; English (UK): `bf_emma`; Spanish: `ef_dora`, `em_alex`; French: `ff_siwis`; Portuguese (Brazil): `pf_dora`; Italian: `if_sara`; Japanese: `jf_alpha`; Chinese: `zf_xiaobei`; Hindi: `hf_alpha` |

Phones get the same catalog. Files come from
[simonfxr/kokoro.cpp-GGUF](https://huggingface.co/simonfxr/kokoro.cpp-GGUF)
on Hugging Face into the directory given to `init`, named as in the catalog
(`kokoro-82m-q8_0.gguf`, `kokoro-voice-af_heart.gguf`); copying them there
works too.

- `download(id)`: a model or a voice id. Blocks (call it from a worker),
  emits "tts:download" (`{ id, done_mb, total_mb }`, `done_mb == total_mb`
  at the end) and returns at once when the file is already there. The file
  is checked against its SHA-256 before it is renamed into place; a
  mismatch deletes it and returns `error.ChecksumMismatch`.
- `delete(id)`: removes a model or voice (and a half-finished download of
  it); a loaded model is unloaded first, `error.Speaking` while it is in use.
- `status()`: the catalog with what is on the device, the loaded model,
  where it runs (`backend`, `gpu`), the phoneme-data directory, the
  download in progress, and the current phase.

# Speaking

`speak(a, text, opts)` reads `text` aloud and returns when it has been
played or stopped. It blocks: call it from an async command. A new `speak`
or a `stop` (from any thread) silences the current utterance at once, and
its `speak` returns promptly with `stopped = true`.

`Options`:

| Field | Default | |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `model` | `"auto"` | A `models` id; `"auto"`: the default if downloaded, else any. |
| `voice` | `"auto"` | A `voices` id; `"auto"`: the first voice on the device for the language (then one of the same family, then any). |
| `lang` | `"auto"` | An espeak-ng language (`"en-us"`, `"es"`, `"fr"`...); `"auto"`: guessed from the text (`guessLanguage`). |
| `speed` | `1.0` | 0.25-4 (2 = twice as fast). |
| `markdown` | `true` | Read Markdown as prose (`prepareText`: headings, lists, emphasis, code fences and link targets removed). |
| `backend` | `.auto` | `.auto`: the GPU when ggml has one (CUDA, Vulkan, Metal), else the CPU; `.cpu`. |
| `threads` | `0` | CPU threads; 0: `defaultThreads()` (up to 8 on desktops, a phone's fast cores, 2 to 4). |

`Result`: `voice`, `lang` and `backend` it was read with (allocated with
`a`), `chunks`, `first_audio_ms` (from the call to the first sound, model
loading included), `synth_ms`, `load_ms` (0 when the model was loaded),
`audio_s` (`synth_ms / 1000 / audio_s` is the real-time factor), `gap_ms`
(silence heard because synthesis fell behind) and `stopped`.

"tts:state" follows each utterance: `{ phase, message, voice }`, with
`phase` one of `"loading"`, `"generating"`, `"playing"`, `"idle"` (message
`"Stopped"` or `"Finished"`) or `"error"` (message: the error's name).

Errors include `error.NotInitialized`, `error.NoModel` / `error.NoVoice`
(nothing downloaded), `error.UnknownModel` / `error.UnknownVoice`,
`error.EspeakDataMissing` and `error.ModelLoadFailed`.

How it streams: the text is cut into chunks at sentence, clause or word
boundaries (`oriel.tts.chunks`): a short first one so sound starts quickly,
then chunks of up to ~220 bytes sized to the audio already queued. One
`audio_play.Stream` plays the whole utterance while the next chunk is
synthesized, so there are no gaps when synthesis is faster than real time,
and no false "finished" between chunks.

The Kokoro context stays loaded between utterances: another voice or
language reuses it, another model or backend reloads it.

- `warmUp(opts)`: loads the model and voice `speak` would use with `opts`
  without speaking; on a GPU it also synthesizes a word, so pipelines
  compile before the first utterance. Call it from a worker when a reading
  screen opens. `speak` waits for a warm-up in progress.
- `unloadIdle()`: frees the context unless speaking (safe on the main
  thread); call it on memory pressure or when the app goes to the
  background. The next utterance loads it again.
- `deinit()`: releases the context; `App.run` calls it after its queued
  commands drain.
- `guessLanguage(text)`, `prepareText(a, text)` and `defaultThreads()` are
  public for pages that show what Auto will pick.
- `audio_context`: a miniaudio context to play through (null: the default
  device); tests and headless runs point it at miniaudio's null backend.

# Bundled phoneme data

Kokoro phonemizes with espeak-ng, which reads its compiled runtime data
(`espeak-ng-data/`: `phontab`, `phonindex`, `phondata`, `intonations`, one
`<name>_dict` per language and the `lang/` voice definitions). Oriel
compiles it at build time from the espeak-ng 1.52.0 sources with a host tool
(`tools/espeak_data.zig`, wired by `build/espeak_data.zig`), and `addApp`
ships it with every `.kokoro = true` app, about 2.9 MB with the default
languages (half of it Mandarin):

| Platform | Location |
| --- | --- |
| Linux, Windows | `espeak-ng-data/` next to the executable (`zig-out/bin`, the deb/rpm's `/usr/lib/<app>/`, the AppImage, the NSIS installer) |
| macOS | `<App>.app/Contents/Resources/espeak-ng-data` (linked from `Contents/MacOS`) |
| iOS | `<App>.app/espeak-ng-data` |
| Android | APK assets (`zig-out/android-assets`), copied to `<filesDir>/espeak-ng-data` on first start and after each app update |

The default dictionaries are `en` (en-us, en-gb), `es`, `fr`, `pt` (pt-br),
`it`, `ja`, `cmn` (Mandarin: espeak's `zh`/`cmn` voices) and `hi`. Add
others with the dependency option `tts_languages`, a comma-separated list
of espeak-ng dictionary names (`dictsource/<name>_rules`):

```zig
.tts_languages = "en,es,fr,pt,it,ja,cmn,hi,de,ko",
```

`zig build espeak-data -Dkokoro -Dwhisper` in the Oriel checkout writes the
same directory to `zig-out/espeak-ng-data`.

At run time `oriel.tts` looks for the data once, before the first engine
init: `$KOKORO_ESPEAK_DATA_PATH`, then `<models_dir>/espeak-ng-data`, then
the bundled copy, then the system's (Linux packages, Homebrew). Without any
of them `speak` returns `error.EspeakDataMissing`. Setting
`KOKORO_ESPEAK_DATA_PATH` is optional; use it to point at other data in
espeak-ng 1.52.0's format. On Windows espeak-ng opens the files through
ANSI paths, so the directory's path must be representable in the system's
code page.

**Android projects created before this release** need one line in
`app/build.gradle.kts` (new projects get it from the template; the Kotlin
runtime that extracts the assets is updated on every build), inside
`android { }`, with the path from the app module to your `zig-out`:

```kotlin
sourceSets["main"].assets.srcDirs("../../zig-out/android-assets")
```

# Performance

Measured with the Q8_0 model, streaming (`speak`), warm (model loaded):

| Device | Backend | First audio | Real-time factor |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| RTX 4070 | CUDA | ~54 ms | ~0.02-0.03 |
| RTX 4070 | Vulkan | similar to CUDA | ~0.02-0.03; 1-4 s pipeline compile on a context's first use (`warmUp` hides it) |
| Ryzen 7 7800X3D | CPU, 8 threads | ~0.6 s | ~0.36 |
| Snapdragon 732G | CPU, 2 fast cores | ~4 s | ~2.75: gaps |

A real-time factor under 1 means synthesis runs faster than playback, so
the utterance plays without gaps after the first chunk. Low-end phones
synthesize slower than real time: on a Snapdragon 732G speech comes with
pauses between chunks (`Result.gap_ms` reports them). Phones run Kokoro on
the CPU where Vulkan 1.2 isn't usable (older Adrenos) and Kokoro has no
OpenCL path; there `oriel.tts` uses only the fast cores (a thread on a
little core holds every step back) and shortens the first chunk to what the
measured speed makes in ~1.5 s. Build phone apps with the default
`-Dggml_arm=dotprod`; without it the 732G's RTF was ~7.5. Metal compiles in
on Apple platforms but hasn't been measured yet.

# Building blocks: `oriel.kokoro` and `oriel.audio_play`

For pipelines of your own (writing WAV files, other playback), use the
modules `oriel.tts` is built on. Run model loading and synthesis on a
worker; serialize operations on a context, and keep it until synthesis has
finished. Before the first `kokoro.init`, make the phoneme data reachable:
`oriel.tts` does it for you; without it, set `KOKORO_ESPEAK_DATA_PATH`.

```zig
const params = oriel.kokoro.defaultParams(); // .backend: CPU or AUTO
const ctx = oriel.kokoro.init("models/kokoro-82m-q8_0.gguf", params)
    orelse return error.ModelLoadFailed;
defer oriel.kokoro.free(ctx);
try oriel.kokoro.loadVoice(ctx, "models/kokoro-voice-af_heart.gguf");
try oriel.kokoro.setLanguage(ctx, "en-us");

const audio = try oriel.kokoro.synthesize(ctx, "Hello from Oriel.");
defer oriel.kokoro.freePcm(audio.samples);
try oriel.audio_play.start(io, gpa, audio.samples, audio.rate);
// Registered after freePcm so stop joins callbacks before PCM is freed.
defer oriel.audio_play.stop();
while (!oriel.audio_play.finished()) {
    try io.sleep(.fromMilliseconds(20), .awake);
}
```

Output is mono float32 PCM at 24 kHz. Free it with `freePcm`, which uses the
engine's allocator. `lastError()` returns the C engine's error text after a
model-loading failure. With AUTO, call `oriel.ggml_gpu.load(io)` before
loading a model to register the CUDA or Vulkan backend.

## One-shot playback

`audio_play.start` plays caller-owned PCM; stop playback before freeing its
samples or the allocator used for its device. There is one active
utterance at a time: `start` stops and joins the previous device, and
`finished` can be polled before initialization and after shutdown. `stop`
and `shutdown` are idempotent on a control thread. `App.run` also shuts
playback down after its queued commands drain.

Set `on_progress` and `on_done` before starting playback; change them only
after stopping. They run on the audio thread, and `on_done` reports natural
completion rather than an explicit stop. Queue UI or lifecycle work to
another thread. `start` rejects calls from an audio callback. A callback's
`stop` only silences output; a later control-thread stop/shutdown releases
the device.

## Streaming playback: `audio_play.Stream`

One device for a whole utterance, fed chunk by chunk while it plays,
through a bounded ring (`Stream.buffer_seconds`, 30 s):

```zig
const s = try oriel.audio_play.Stream.init(io, gpa, 24000);
defer s.deinit();          // releases the device: never in its callback
try s.start();             // or startWithContext(ctx): a miniaudio context of yours
try s.append(chunk1);      // copied; blocks while the ring is full
try s.append(chunk2);      // error.Cancelled once stopped
s.seal();                  // no more audio: finished() after the drain
while (!s.finished()) try io.sleep(.fromMilliseconds(20), .awake);
```

`stop` works from any thread, the audio callback included: output turns
silent at once, `append` returns `error.Cancelled` and `finished` is true.
`deinit` stops the stream, releases the device and frees it, on the owning
thread after other threads are done calling `stop`. `playedSeconds`,
`queuedSeconds` and `starvedSeconds` (silence inserted because appends fell
behind) report progress. A `Stream` is independent of the one-shot API.

# Validation

The numbers above come from the devices in the table: Linux on the CPU,
CUDA and Vulkan, and an Android phone's CPU. Playback lifecycle tests use
miniaudio's silent null backend, which does not open the user's audio
device. This does not claim device or synthesis validation on every
supported platform.

To include real synthesis checks in the regression suite, provide local
assets explicitly. The test runner has no bundled phoneme data next to it,
so point `KOKORO_ESPEAK_DATA_PATH` at a copy (`zig build espeak-data
-Dkokoro -Dwhisper` writes one to `zig-out/espeak-ng-data`) unless the
system has espeak-ng's:

```sh
KOKORO_ESPEAK_DATA_PATH=$PWD/zig-out/espeak-ng-data \
ORIEL_TTS_MODEL=/path/to/kokoro-82m-q8_0.gguf \
ORIEL_TTS_VOICE=/path/to/kokoro-voice-af_heart.gguf \
zig build test -Dkokoro -Dwhisper -Daudio_capture=false
```

`ORIEL_TTS_DIR=<dir with kokoro-82m-q8_0.gguf, kokoro-voice-af_heart.gguf
and kokoro-voice-ef_dora.gguf>` adds the streaming test (first audio, RTF
and gaps per backend, English and Spanish; silent unless
`ORIEL_TTS_SPEAKERS=1`). With `-Dggml_vulkan` or `-Dggml_cuda` the test step
installs the GPU library in `zig-out/lib` and the tests load it from there,
so the GPU is measured too (`-Doptimize=ReleaseFast` for real numbers).

# Licenses

The optional TTS stack includes espeak-ng and the runtime data compiled
from it, licensed GPL-3.0-or-later; apps built with `.kokoro = true` ship
that data. Kokoro (kokoro.cpp), Highway, miniaudio and ucd-tools have their
own notices. Oriel's framework license remains MIT; dependency texts are in
`NOTICE` and `licenses/tts`. The model and voice files are downloaded by
the app at run time, not distributed with Oriel; their licenses are
upstream's.
